---
/**
 * /colophon — the front-end design page: what this site is made of and why
 * it feels the way it does. Swatches and specimens render straight from the
 * live design tokens, so this page always tells the truth.
 */
import Base from '@/layouts/Base.astro';

const swatches = [
  { name: 'ground', varName: '--color-ground', note: 'warm gallery white — the resting state' },
  { name: 'surface', varName: '--color-surface', note: 'panels and cards' },
  { name: 'ink', varName: '--color-ink', note: 'soft blue-black text' },
  { name: 'muted', varName: '--color-muted', note: 'margin notes, secondary text' },
  { name: 'line', varName: '--color-line', note: 'hairlines, frames, ticks' },
  { name: 'accent', varName: '--color-accent', note: 'archival stamp cobalt — links, focus' },
  { name: 'signal', varName: '--color-signal', note: 'photo-chemical amber — small markers' },
];
---

<Base
  title="Colophon"
  description="How this site is designed and built: palette, type, the index-rail signature, and the files-as-content model."
>
  <div class="wrap max-w-4xl py-10">
    <header>
      <p class="kicker">colophon</p>
      <h1 class="mt-2 font-display text-4xl sm:text-5xl">How this site is made</h1>
      <p class="mt-3 max-w-prose text-lg leading-relaxed text-muted">
        A portfolio should feel like the person. This one is built to feel like a contact
        sheet someone annotated by hand — photographs first, quiet margin notes underneath,
        nothing shouting.
      </p>
    </header>

    <section class="mt-12" aria-labelledby="idea-h">
      <p class="kicker">the idea</p>
      <h2 id="idea-h" class="mt-1 font-display text-2xl">Frames and figures</h2>
      <div class="prose-reflection mt-4">
        <p>
          I make film frames and I read financial figures, so the design borrows from both:
          large imagery carries the pages, and a single mono-spaced “index rail” annotates
          everything the way a timecode annotates footage — or a ticker annotates a price.
          Every entry gets an archival code (<span class="rail">E-014</span>), every date is a
          timestamp, and the timeline reads like a reel.
        </p>
        <p>
          That’s the one deliberately bold element. Everything else stays comfortable on
          purpose: warm paper tones, soft corners, generous space, and motion kept to a
          gentle page reveal that switches itself off when your system asks for reduced
          motion.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-12" aria-labelledby="palette-h">
      <p class="kicker">palette</p>
      <h2 id="palette-h" class="mt-1 font-display text-2xl">Colour, from the darkroom</h2>
      <p class="mt-3 max-w-prose text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted">
        Rendered live from the design tokens — flip the theme toggle and this page re-grades
        itself. Cobalt is the stamp on the back of an archival print; amber is photo-chemical.
        (Deliberately not the default cream-and-terracotta or black-and-acid-green looks.)
      </p>
      <ul class="mt-5 grid list-none gap-3 p-0 sm:grid-cols-2">
        {
          swatches.map((s) => (
            <li class="panel flex items-center gap-4 p-3">
              <span
                class="size-12 shrink-0 rounded-(--radius-chip) border border-line"
                style={`background: var(${s.varName})`}
                aria-hidden="true"
              />
              <span class="min-w-0">
                <span class="rail block">{s.varName}</span>
                <span class="block text-sm text-muted">{s.note}</span>
              </span>
            </li>
          ))
        }
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-12" aria-labelledby="type-h">
      <p class="kicker">type</p>
      <h2 id="type-h" class="mt-1 font-display text-2xl">Three voices</h2>
      <div class="mt-5 space-y-4">
        <div class="panel p-5">
          <p class="rail">Instrument Serif — display, used sparingly</p>
          <p class="mt-2 font-display text-3xl leading-snug">
            The director’s cut of a CV, <em>with feelings kept in</em>.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="panel p-5">
          <p class="rail">Inter — body, built for reading</p>
          <p class="mt-2 max-w-prose leading-relaxed">
            Reflections are the heart of every entry, so the body face stays out of the way.
            Chinese renders inline without breaking anything: 夾單, 組長, 香港 —
            <span lang="zh-Hant">粵語都得</span>.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="panel p-5">
          <p class="rail">IBM Plex Mono — the index rail</p>
          <p class="mt-2 font-mono text-sm">E-014 · 2026-04 → 2026-06 · SEMI-FINALIST · HKG</p>
          <div class="reel-ticks mt-3" aria-hidden="true"></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-12" aria-labelledby="model-h">
      <p class="kicker">under the hood</p>
      <h2 id="model-h" class="mt-1 font-display text-2xl">Content is just files</h2>
      <div class="prose-reflection mt-4">
        <p>
          Built with Astro, TypeScript, and Tailwind tokens; no database, no CMS. Entries and
          logs are Markdown files, categories and periods are two tiny data files, and every
          page derives from them — adding a tab, a season, an entry, or a monthly log means
          adding a file, never touching code. Images are optimized at build time; the whole
          site ships as static pages.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>
  </div>
</Base>
